msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.TokenizerInfo.decoded_vocab:1
msgid ""
"The decoded vocabulary of the tokenizer. This converts the tokens in the "
"LLM's vocabulary back to the original format of the input text. E.g. for "
"type ByteFallback, the token <0x1B> is converted back to \"\"."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.dump_metadata:1
msgid ""
"Dump the metadata of the tokenizer to a json string. It can be used to "
"construct the tokenizer info from the vocabulary and the metadata string."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_huggingface:1
msgid ""
"Construct the tokenizer info from the huggingface tokenizer. This "
"constructor supports various tokenizer backends, including the "
"huggingface fast tokenizer and tiktoken tokenizer. Necessary information "
"is automatically detected from the tokenizer."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_huggingface:5
msgid ""
"Note that some models (e.g. Phi-3 and Deepseek-V2) may pad the vocabulary"
" to a multiple of 32. In this case, the model's vocab_size is larger than"
" the tokenizer's vocabulary size. Please pass the model's vocab_size "
"(this should be defined in the model config) to the vocab_size parameter "
"in the constructor, because this information is used to determine the "
"size of the token mask."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_huggingface:11
msgid ""
"Some models can have more than one stop token ids, and auto detection may"
" not find all of them. In this case, you can specify the stop token ids "
"manually."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_huggingface:14
msgid "The huggingface tokenizer."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_huggingface:23
msgid "**tokenizer_info** -- The tokenizer info."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.TokenizerInfo.from_vocab_and_metadata:6
msgid "The metadata string in json format."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.TokenizerInfo.special_token_ids:1
msgid ""
"The special token ids. Special tokens include control tokens, reserved "
"tokens, padded tokens, etc. Now it is automatically detected from the "
"vocabulary."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:1
msgid ""
"The type of the vocabulary. Used in TokenizerInfo. XGrammar supports "
"three types of vocabularies:"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:4
msgid "RAW"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:5
msgid ""
"The vocabulary is in the raw format. The tokens in the vocabulary are "
"kept in their original form without any processing. This kind of "
"tokenizer includes the tiktoken tokenizer, e.g. microsoft/Phi-3-small-8k-"
"instruct, Qwen/Qwen-7B-Chat, etc."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:9
msgid "BYTE_FALLBACK"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:10
msgid ""
"The vocabulary used in the byte fallback BPE tokenizer. The tokens are "
"encoded through the byte-fallback conversion. E.g. \"\" -> \"<0x1B>\", "
"\" apple\" -> \"▁apple\". This kind of tokenizer includes meta-"
"llama/Llama-2-7b-chat, microsoft/Phi-3.5-mini-instruct, etc."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:14
msgid "BYTE_LEVEL"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:15
msgid ""
"The vocabulary used in the byte level BPE tokenizer. The tokens are "
"encoded through the byte-to-unicode conversion, as in "
"https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/87be06ca77166e6a6215eee5a990ab9f07238a18/src/transformers/models/gpt2/tokenization_gpt2.py#L38-L59"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.tokenizer_info.VocabType:19
msgid ""
"This kind of tokenizer includes meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct, "
"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, etc."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.allocate_token_bitmask:1
msgid ""
"Allocate the bitmask for the next token prediction. The bitmask is an "
"int32 tensor on CPU with shape (batch_size, ceil(vocab_size / 32)). Users"
" who have their own needs to manage CUDA memory can construct the tensor "
"with get_bitmask_shape and bitmask_dtype themselves."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.allocate_token_bitmask:6
msgid ""
"The reason why we use int32 instead of uint32 is that old versions of "
"PyTorch do not support uint32."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.allocate_token_bitmask:9
msgid "The batch size of the bitmask."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.allocate_token_bitmask:14
msgid "**bitmask** -- The shape of the bitmask."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:1
msgid ""
"Apply the bitmask to the logits in-place. The bitmask is a 01 bitwise "
"compressed tensor, where 0 means the token is masked and 1 means the "
"token is not masked. It can be generated by allocate_token_bitmask and "
"filled by fill_next_token_bitmask. After applying the bitmask, the masked"
" logits will be set to -inf."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:6
msgid ""
"The shape of logits and bitmask should be (batch_size, vocab_size) and "
"(batch_size, bitmask_size) respectively. bitmask_size = ceil(vocab_size /"
" 32). The operation is:"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:16
msgid ""
"get_bitmask_value(bitmask, i, j) gets the j-th bit of the i-th row of the"
" bitmask."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:18
msgid ""
"Indices can be used to specify which logits in the batch to apply the "
"bitmask to. It is especially useful when there are structured requests "
"and unstructured requests mixed in the same batch by skipping masking the"
" logits in the unstructured requests. When specified, the operation will "
"be"
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:30
msgid ""
"The logits and bitmask should be on the same device. If both them are on "
"CUDA, we launch a CUDA kernel to apply bitmask. If both them are on CPU, "
"we use a CPU implementation. The CUDA kernel is optimized and should be "
"preferred."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:34
msgid ""
"In practice, the bitmask is allocated on CPU, and the logits is usually "
"on GPU, so users should manually copy the bitmask to GPU before calling "
"this function."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:37
msgid "The tensor to apply the bitmask to."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:39
msgid "The bitmask to apply."
msgstr ""

#: of xgrammar.matcher.apply_token_bitmask_inplace:41
msgid ""
"A list of indices to specify which logits in the batch to apply the "
"bitmask to. If None, apply the bitmask to all logits in the batch."
msgstr ""

